  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nThe latest surface analysis has a high over Michigan and frontal \nboundary stationary extending southwest from the mid Atlantic \nthrough the Southeast to Texas. An upper level wave will pass \nthrough the Dakotas today and through MN/WI/MI tonight. The southern \nextent of precipitation with this trough will be as far south as \nperhaps the IL/WI border overnight tonight. There will be a cold \nfront associated with this upper feature, but lagging behind a bit. \nThis will allow highs to rise a bit more on Sunday (70s) than today \n(70-75) in most areas, except perhaps the northwest. Expect \nhighs to be a bit cooler on Monday, back to around 70 or low \n70s. The coolest night of the week will be Monday night as cool \nhigh pressure will dominate...allowing lows to dip into the \nlower 40s across east- central IL. A few upper 30s readings \nwouldn't be out of the question either.\n\nWith the jet stream to the north extending from the southern prairie \nprovinces through the Great Lakes, weather system tracks will remain \nto the north of IL through much of the week. The next system to \npotentially affect the area looks to be on Thursday night. But then, \nit's a weak wave and could perhaps bring some showers to northern \nIL. Right now, the chance this wave affects central Illinois is \nunder 10%, so it won't be included in the PoPs forecast right now. \n\nTemperatures look to warm above average for the latter part of the \nweek with the jet stream staying north. Currently expecting highs \naround 80 for Wed-Fri, with perhaps mid 80s by Saturday. The biggest \nquestion in the forecast is at day 7-8 when models diverge a bit. \nThere is the potential for an upper trough to deepen over the \nwestern US while a weak TX upper low ejects northeastward ahead of \nthe trough. This is the next big chance for rain over IL...Sunday \nnight into Monday. While a pattern shift is possible late in the \nforecast period, it may hold off to the following week.",
